**`/app/answer.sql`** — joins orders to products, filters to Q4 2024 (`>= 2024-10-01 AND < 2025-01-01`), sums `quantity * unit_price` per `(category, product_id)`, then ranks with `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY total_sales DESC, product_id)` and keeps `rn <= 3`.

It emits exactly `category, product_id, total_sales` with no NULL rows, so the `groupby('category')['product_id'].apply(list)` extraction gives three ids per category.

**Check:** the consumer script was run verbatim against duckdb 1.3.2 / pandas 2.1.4 in the repo venv (`/app/.venv`). It executed cleanly and returned `{'Clothing': [67, 78, 44], 'Electronics': [21, 94, 81], 'Grocery': [85, 84, 22], 'Home': [3, 64, 88], 'Toys': [96, 28, 15]}`; an independent pandas groupby cross-check over the Q4 slice produced the identical dict, and per-category totals came back in descending order. All five categories had ≥3 products with Q4 sales, so no short lists or nulls.

Nothing left undone; the CSVs were not modified.

